{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>The enemy said.</b> To his people — when he schemed with words: 'I will pursue and overtake them, and I will divide spoil with my officers and my servants.'" "<b>תמלאמו.</b> It will be filled from them — my spirit, my breath, and my will. And do not wonder at a word that speaks in two forms: תִּמְלָאֵמוֹ — תִּמָּלֵא מֵהֶם. There are many like this in the language: ״כִּי אֶרֶץ הַנֶּגֶב נְתַתָּנִי״ (Judges 1:15) — like 'you gave me'; ״וְלֹא יָכְלוּ דַּבְּרוֹ לְשָׁלוֹם״ (Genesis 37:4) — like 'speak with him'; ״בָּנַי יְצָאוּנִי״ (Jeremiah 10:20) — like 'they went out from me'; ״מִסְפַּר צְעָדַי אֲגִידֶנּוּ״ (Job 31:37) — like 'I will tell him.' So too here — תִּמְלָאֵמוֹ: my soul will be filled from them." "<b>אריק חרבי.</b> I will draw. And because one empties the blade when drawing it and it is left empty — the sense of הֲרָקָה applies, as in ״מְרִיקִים שַׂקֵּיהֶם״ (Genesis 42:35), ״וְכֵלָיו יָרִיקוּ״ (Jeremiah 48:12). And do not say the sense of emptiness applies only to what comes out — rather, to the sheath, the sack, and the vessel from which it came out, but not to the sword or the wine — and force a reading of ״אָרִיק חַרְבִּי״ in the sense of ״וַיָּרֶק אֶת חֲנִיכָיו״ (Genesis 14:14), 'I will arm myself with my sword.' We find the term applied also to what comes out: ״שֶׁמֶן תּוּרַק״ (Song of Songs 1:3); ״וְלֹא הוּרַק מִכְּלִי אֶל כֶּלִי״ (Jeremiah 48:11) — it is not written 'the vessel was not emptied,' but 'the wine was not poured from vessel to vessel.' We find the term applied to the wine. And likewise, ״וְהֵרִיקוּ חַרְבוֹתָם עַל יְפִי חָכְמָתֶךָ״ — they will draw their swords (Ezekiel 28:7)." "<b>תורישמו.</b> A term of dispossession and impoverishment, as in ״מוֹרִישׁ וּמַעֲשִׁיר״ (I Samuel 2)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:52:30.314891672Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}